    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I’ve always seen math as a tool to understand the world, especially in physics and economics. I use it daily in my work, though I don’t see myself as a mathematician. My background in physics helped me appreciate patterns and logic, and I enjoy solving problems that feel intuitive, like balancing equations or modeling real-world systems.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Not really. I’ve never felt anxious about math because I’ve learned to approach it as a process rather than a test. If I’m stuck, I break it down—like climbing a rock wall. I focus on understanding, not speed. My low neuroticism helps; I stay calm even when problems seem complex.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "Yes, I used an AI tool last year to review calculus concepts. It walked me through derivations step by step, which helped me visualize functions and their behavior. It wasn’t perfect, but it made abstract ideas more concrete. I found it especially useful for checking my work and catching mistakes.",
